WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.040
There are lots of good reasons
to try and follow a healthier diet—

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.120
you lose weight,
you feel good,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.920
but the main reason?

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:13.440
To live a longer, happier,
more productive life.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.520
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.480
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.400
Back by popular demand,
it’s the NutritionFacts Grab Bag,

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.200
where we look at the latest science
on a whole variety of topics.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.600
And we start with Ginkgo Biloba.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.000
Turns out it doesn’t seem to have any effect in
preventing cognitive decline or dementia.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.200
But it may play a role in treating it.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.320
More than a third
of older Americans

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.120
take some kind
of brain health supplement

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.400
to the tune
of billions of dollars a year,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.080
and one of the most popular
is ginkgo biloba.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.040
U.S. household surveys suggest

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.600
as many as 2 percent
of Americans

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.760
are taking gingko supplements.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.960
Ginkgo biloba's a tree
with fan-shaped leaves,

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.920
regarded as a living fossil,

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.200
having persisted
relatively unchanged

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.840
for over
a quarter billion years.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.440
Its resilience makes it
a favorite of urban planners,

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.400
hardy enough to survive
even a nuclear blast,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.640
as one of the few survivors
within a short radius

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.920
of ground zero in Hiroshima.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.080
Over the last 30 years or so,
an extract of gingko leaves

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.000
has become one of the most
widely used herbal treatments

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.680
for dementia.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.560
A 2009 Cochrane review

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.000
of three dozen
randomized controlled trials

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.640
involving more than
4,000 participants

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.880
concluded that
the "evidence that ginkgo biloba

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.320
has predictable and clinically
significant benefit

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.880
for people with dementia
or cognitive impairment

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.880
is inconsistent
and unreliable."

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.360
One trial showed
very large benefits,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.280
but others showed
no effect at all.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.920
To put the subject to rest
once and for all,

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.360
two large randomized,
double-blind,

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.320
placebo-controlled trials
were conducted, one in Europe,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.680
funded by a gingko
supplement manufacturer,

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.720
and the largest,
longest study to date,

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.320
funded by the NIH
in the United States.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.800
More than 5,000
older men and women

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:12.360
with either normal cognition
or mild cognitive impairment

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.960
were randomized to
a gingko leaf extract or placebo

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.000
for five or six years
to see if it would prevent them

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.760
from sliding into dementia.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.120
Mild cognitive impairment

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.400
is the intermediate
clinical state

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.440
between normal cognition
for one's age and dementia.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.600
The conversion rate
of MCI to dementia

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.400
is about 10 percent per year.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.000
The bottom line?

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.800
Neither trial
showed any clear benefit,

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.400
definitively demonstrating
no preventative effect.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.200
However, for those
already suffering with dementia,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.600
gingko leaf extracts do appear
to slow cognitive decline

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.520
compared to placebo
for those with Alzheimer's,

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.760
or dementia
more broadly defined.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.520
Of course that's assuming
there's actually gingko

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.640
in your gingko supplements.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.520
Investigations
of gingko supplements

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.640
off the shelves
in both Europe and the U.S.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.880
found some that had
no apparent gingko at all.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.520
As high as about
one in six ginkgo supplements

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.200
appeared to be all filler,

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.480
without any detectable
ginkgo biloba DNA.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.000
Side-effects-wise,
at typical doses,

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.320
such as 120 mg twice a day,

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.480
ginkgo extracts may cause
mild stomach upset,

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.960
headache, dizziness,
constipation,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.040
and allergic skin reactions.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.920
Higher dosages can result
in restlessness, diarrhea,

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.320
nausea, vomiting, and weakness.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.400
There have been about a dozen
published case reports

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.920
of bleeding
associated with gingko,

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:44.840
but thankfully
this did not surface

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.920
in systematic reviews
of the dementia studies.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.000
Still, out of
an abundance of caution,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.320
it's been recommended to
stop taking gingko supplements

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:57.160
at least two weeks
before elective surgery.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.840
The bottom line,
according to a recent review

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.640
of Alzheimer's disease
therapies,

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.520
is that ginkgo
is one of the few things

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.240
that can beat out placebo
for cognitive function.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.040
In our next story,

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.480
we look at Is the higher rate of
bladder cancer among hairdressers.

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:15.760
And we ask: is it due to
exposure to hair dyes?

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.880
And what about at-home
use of hair colorants?

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:23.280
Since there's typically no way
to reverse hair color loss,

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.800
the use of hair colorants
is a mainstay of management,

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.160
a practice documented
as far back as 1500 BCE.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.840
While gray hair may carry
a distinguished look

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.760
and limit women's subjection
to a sexualized gaze,

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.160
hair color constitutes
a multibillion-dollar market

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.160
with up to 60 percent
of men and women

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.200
in Western countries choosing
to use coloring products,

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.400
many of which to cover gray.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.400
For over a century,
aromatic amines

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.080
have been
the main chemicals used

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.480
in commercial permanent,
oxidative hair dyes.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.440
The primary problem
with these compounds

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.760
is that they can be
potent contact allergens.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.320
Product labels often recommend
a small 48-hour test application

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.200
to the skin before use,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.120
but it can take up to a week
for a skin allergy to flare up.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.920
They also may cause cancer.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.440
Hair products contain
more than 5,000 chemicals,

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.440
and so it's hard
to narrow it down,

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.120
but the aromatic amines,
such as para-phenylenediamine,

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:26.800
that are often used in hair dyes

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:28.880
to prevent fading
due to washing,

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.360
have been picked out
as a "probable carcinogen."

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.720
Before industry reformulation

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.080
in response to
mandated cancer warning labels,

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.360
9 out of 10 permanent
hair dye products

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:40.680
were mutagenic,

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.800
meaning capable
of causing DNA mutations.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:47.080
This then appeared to translate
into more than ten times

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.800
the odds of DNA damage
in breast cells

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.320
obtained from
the breast milk of women

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:53.920
who had used hair dye
in the last six months,

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.400
compared to
not using hair dyes at all.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.640
But does it translate
into more cancer?

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:04.480
A meta-analysis of 42 studies
found that hairdressers,

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.240
particularly those
who held their jobs

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.160
10 or more years, were at
significantly higher risk

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:12.280
of developing bladder cancer
later in life.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.560
Hairdressers, beauticians,
and barbers

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.360
may also suffer
more lung cancer,

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:19.480
voice box cancer,
and multiple myeloma.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:23.080
There is some evidence that
hairdressers may smoke more,

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.880
but the researchers
took this into account.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.880
They conclude that salons
should be better ventilated,

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:30.920
that gloves should be used.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:32.760
But how much is that
going to help the person

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.560
whose head
has been soaked in it?

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.800
Based on testing
for radioactively-traced

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.200
hair dye ingredients
in the urine,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.520
experiments suggest that
exposure to hair dye

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.160
may be
several hundred-fold higher

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.200
in hair dye users
than the occupational risk

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:49.760
to the hairdressers.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:53.040
But thankfully there does not
seem to be an excess risk

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:54.800
of bladder cancer
among those who just

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:56.920
use hair dye personally.

00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:00.080
Furthermore, major changes
took place in the 1980s

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.440
to make hair dyes safer.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.720
In 1979, the U.S.
Food and Drug Administration

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:08.200
started requiring
cancer warning labels

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.800
on hair dye products,

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:13.040
leading the industry to start
reformulating to eliminate

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:15.960
the most carcinogenic
ingredients.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:18.160
A turning point
in bladder cancer risk

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:19.760
isn't apparent from the data,

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:22.520
but this may just be a function
of the long latency time

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:25.720
between exposure and malignancy
for bladder cancer,

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.880
which can be 30 or 40 years.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.320
If you look at
faster developing cancers,

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.840
such as leukemia
or follicular lymphoma,

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.880
there does seem to be
a drop-off in risk after 1980,

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:39.920
though a 2019 meta-analysis,
looking at

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.520
increased
non-Hodgkin's lymphoma risk

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.160
without consideration
of date range,

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.040
concluded "exposure to hair
colorants should be reduced

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.240
as much as possible."

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.440
For prostate cancer,
the doubling of odds

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:55.000
associated with hair dye
use seems limited

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.400
to pre-1980 exposure,

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.520
and there appears to be
no link to brain tumors

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.480
in either time frame.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.240
As a tragically ironic aside,

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.760
while the risk associated with
hair products may have declined

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.720
for white men and women,
the opposite trend

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.880
seems to have occurred
for certain hair products

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:14.440
targeted towards
African Americans.

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:16.320
The Black Women's Health Study,

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.640
which assessed exposure
in the 1990s,

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.280
found no association
between breast cancer

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.240
and the use of hair "relaxers,"

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:25.880
chemical treatments
used to straighten hair.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:27.680
But in the early 2000s,

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:29.440
popular straighteners
switched from

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.320
non-carcinogenic compounds,
like lye,

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.000
to chemical cocktails
containing formaldehyde.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:38.320
So now,
frequent straightener use

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:39.680
is associated with

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:42.520
about a 30 percent higher
breast cancer risk.

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:44.480
Thankfully, there's been
a shift towards embracing

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.800
more natural hair styles in
the African-American community,

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:49.760
resulting in
the sales of hair relaxers

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.280
falling by 40 percent
in recent years.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:55.280
Finally today,

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.080
how do you treat the
cause of bad breath?

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:02.080
Bad breath is an
underestimated problem affecting

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:04.960
a significant proportion
of the global population.

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:07.560
It seems most people
regularly report

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:10.040
encountering people
with halitosis.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:13.160
Masking agents are frequently
used to cover up bad breath,

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:14.360
but studies have shown
that the use

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:16.800
of mouth rinses, sprays,
or mints containing

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.440
an agreeable masking odor

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:19.840
only have a short-term effect,

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:22.100
since you're not treating
the underlying cause,

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:24.800
so the exclusive usage
of masking products

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:26.880
can never be truly considered

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.360
an effective solution
to the problem.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:33.880
The cause of bad breath
is oral putrefaction,

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:35.760
the degradation
of certain amino acids

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.720
mostly derived
by bacterial breakdown

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:39.680
of proteins in your mouth.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:41.240
Specifically,
bacteria degrade

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:42.920
the sulfur-containing
amino acids,

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:44.480
such as cysteine
and methionine,

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:45.760
into stinky compounds

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:48.080
like the rotten egg gas
hydrogen sulfide,

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:51.040
so-called
volatile sulfur compounds.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:54.080
If you measure these compounds
spewing out of people's mouths

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:55.880
with machines
with names like Breathtron,

00:09:55.880 --> 00:10:00.040
tooth brushing alone
does not significantly reduce

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:01.840
oral malodor.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:04.240
Brushing alone reduces
sulfur breath compounds

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:07.120
from more than 450
down to about 300,

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:11.200
but that's still above the
stinky breath threshold of 250.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.000
Add tongue cleaning

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.880
and you can drop that down
to right around the threshold,

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:17.680
but adding
an antiseptic mouthwash--

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:21.240
an antibacterial mouthwash--
did even better.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:24.760
Okay, so what's
the best mouthwash to use?

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:28.800
To find out, researchers used
the cysteine challenge test.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.520
Instead of giving people
dairy products,

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:31.960
which contain
the protein casein,

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:34.400
which is rich in the sulfur
amino acid cysteine,

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:35.760
they just had the participants

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:37.960
swish directly
with some cysteine

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:41.760
and then followed effectiveness
using odor-ograms.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:43.600
For example, toothbrushing.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.360
When people
swished with cysteine

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:46.440
before brushing their teeth,

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:48.640
here's the spike
in stinky sulfur compounds

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:50.520
spewing out of their mouths.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:52.520
When they brushed their teeth
with water

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:55.320
and swished again at various
times over the rest of the day,

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:57.120
the spikes pretty much
looked the same,

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.920
meaning toothbrushing with water
didn't remove

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:01.160
many bad breath bacteria,

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:03.440
since the subjects were
spewing out similar spikes

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.720
starting just 20 minutes later.

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.200
Brushing with toothpaste
works better.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.080
See how it knocks down the
next spike of stinky compounds

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:15.360
before the bacteria slowly
grow back throughout the day?

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:17.378
What about using
a tongue scraper?

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:19.880
Tongue scraping knocks it down
by about half

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:22.880
before coming back
over the next few hours.

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:24.537
What about chewing gum?

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:29.000
Some effect that disappears
over the subsequent few hours.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:30.920
What about various mouthwashes?

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:34.160
First of all,
rinsing with water.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:35.760
Useless.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.360
Can be helpful
for morning breath,

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.880
to rinse any stagnation
that occurred overnight,

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:41.880
but during the day,
just rinsing with water

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.800
isn't clearing
many bad breath bacteria.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.720
And swishing
with Scope mouthwash?

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:50.920
Just as useless as water.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:53.320
Scope is
an anti-bacterial mouthwash

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:54.840
but it must not be good
at killing

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.600
the specific bacteria
that cause bad breath.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.600
Peridex, though, cleaned house,

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:02.760
killing off those bacteria
even seven hours later.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:04.200
What is Peridex?

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.985
That's just a brand name
for chlorhexidine.

00:12:08.026 --> 00:12:10.160
Chlorhexidine doesn't just get
rid of bad breath bacteria;

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.560
it also kills off
the good bacteria on your tongue

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:14.360
that help you make nitric oxide,

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:15.760
which protects your arteries,

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.960
and so chlorhexidine can raise
your blood pressure.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:19.880
And that's not all.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.920
Chlorhexidine
can stain your teeth.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:26.200
That's not what you want
in a mouthwash.

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:28.760
Adverse effects are not rare.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:32.200
Tooth staining, the buildup
of tartar, taste disturbance,

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:34.760
and damage to the lining of the
mouth have all been reported

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:38.000
with the use of chlorhexidine
in about 9 out of 10 people.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:41.080
88 percent of people suffer
various side effects.

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:42.760
But hey, chlorhexidine
may be better

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.680
than mouthwashes
with hydrogen peroxide,

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:47.560
which may be carcinogenic,

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:50.560
potentially increasing
your risk of mouth cancer.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:53.360
That's not just based
on studies on rodents, either.

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.520
Cultures of human
mouth lining cells

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:58.400
show significant DNA damage.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:01.560
Triclosan was another
common antibacterial ingredient

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:02.480
in mouthwashes,

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:06.440
now effectively banned
in over-the-counter products.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:08.800
Tongue scraping alone
doesn't always resolve

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:10.120
the problem of halitosis.

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:12.360
So, what can we use?

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:13.720
If bad breath persists

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:16.000
after improving tongue
and dental hygiene,

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:19.720
an active zinc-based mouth rinse
can be introduced.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:22.640
Zinc binds directly to
the volatile sulfur compounds

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:25.480
and prevents them from
gassing out of your mouth.

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:28.600
Mouth rinses containing
ZnCl without alcohol

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:31.160
have been found to instantly
though temporarily

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:34.000
be effective for halitosis.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:36.320
Researchers looked
at eight mouth rinses,

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:39.280
and the most effective
was a zinc chloride one

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:42.120
and the least effective
was Colgate Plax.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:44.560
In fact, both of the alcohol
rinses were found to be

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:45.960
the least effective.

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:48.480
The thought is that they
may exacerbate halitosis

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:50.857
by drying up your mouth.

